//!    state and the delimiters and environments in the line.
//! 5. If the line is too long, it is wrapped, and both parts are
//!    placed back onto the queue for processing.
//! 6. Otherwise the indentation is applied and the line is added
//!    to the output.
//!
//! When the queue is empty, the next line of the original file is
//! read into it. Processing finishes when both the queue and the
//! original file are exhausted.
//!
//! At the end of the file, warnings are emitted if the indentation
//! does not return to zero, or if any line was negatively indented.
//! Finally, trailing spaces and trailing blank lines are removed.
//!
//! Indentation levels are stored as small signed integers, and the
//! number of indentation characters for a line is the product of
//! its visual indentation level with the tab size.
//!
//! Environments are indented by one level, except for list
//! environments, which are indented by two levels so that the
//! lines following an `\\item` appear indented relative to it.
//! Environments listed as non-indented (by default `document`)
//! do not change the indentation.
//!
//! Delimiters `{`, `(` and `[` increase the indentation by one
//! level, and `}`, `)` and `]` decrease it, with any leading closing
//! delimiters causing the line itself to be dedented.
